WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:15.348
Robert Ramirez - "How I Became a Scientist"

00:00:15.349 --> 00:00:19.452
I'm very pleased to be invited to this forum

00:00:19.453 --> 00:00:23.923
to talk about my experience on becoming a scientist and an educator.

00:00:23.924 --> 00:00:29.962
My name is Robert Ramirez, and I'm a professor of microbiology at San Francisco State University.

00:00:29.963 --> 00:00:35.902
So, what are the lessons that I learned and that I hope to share in this presentation?

00:00:35.903 --> 00:00:40.940
I hope to remind you that with persistence and motivation,

00:00:40.941 --> 00:00:44.944
a career in science and education is open to anyone.

00:00:44.945 --> 00:00:50.816
But, success also depends on possessing a variety of skills.

00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:56.022
First of all, you should study each challenge very carefully—

00:00:56.023 --> 00:00:58.524
you should get as much information as possible.

00:00:58.525 --> 00:01:05.031
You should make very critical decisions.  You should develop a good gut instinct.

00:01:05.032 --> 00:01:06.999
And you should be independent.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:12.705
And not surprisingly, many of these skills are necessary to practice good science.

00:01:12.706 --> 00:01:17.410
I was born in a small agricultural town in northern California.

00:01:17.411 --> 00:01:23.482
I had a very happy childhood of running in the fields and climbing the trees behind my house.

00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:28.454
My mom was a homemaker.  She was born here in the United States

00:01:28.455 --> 00:01:34.126
and graduated from high school.  My father, on the other hand, was a Mexican national

00:01:34.127 --> 00:01:41.534
He was also illiterate.  He could not read or write in Spanish or in English.

00:01:41.535 --> 00:01:48.241
Consequently, my father had to take jobs working in the fields.

00:01:48.242 --> 00:01:52.912
This resulted in a very low income in our household,

00:01:52.913 --> 00:01:57.683
and we were—for lack of a better word—very poor.

00:01:57.684 --> 00:02:04.257
In addition, there was no tradition of education in my family.

00:02:04.258 --> 00:02:08.895
This wasn't because of any negative association with education,

00:02:08.896 --> 00:02:12.899
it was just simply because my parents didn't know anything about it.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:17.937
For economic reasons, my father decided to move my family to Mexico,

00:02:17.938 --> 00:02:20.606
where he could secure a better living.

00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:27.246
The town that he moved us to was a very small, rural town that lacked many services,

00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:35.354
including a high school.  So, at the age of 14, my education came to a stop.

00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:43.062
For four years, I worked on the family ranch, and finally I decided this isn't very good,

00:02:43.063 --> 00:02:46.599
and I decided to return to the United States.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:50.102
Once here in the United States, I got my GED diploma

00:02:50.103 --> 00:02:53.172
and I started attending a community college.

00:02:53.173 --> 00:03:00.446
I started to take every course that I could find that had the word basic in it—

00:03:00.447 --> 00:03:06.052
basic mathematics, basic English, basic chemistry.

00:03:06.053 --> 00:03:09.856
Eventually, after four years of study, my advisor told me

00:03:09.857 --> 00:03:13.926
that it was time for me to transfer to a four-year university

00:03:13.927 --> 00:03:15.828
to get a bachelor's degree,

00:03:15.829 --> 00:03:20.867
and I was, quite honestly, surprised that I was even allowed to do that.

00:03:20.868 --> 00:03:25.838
I transferred to the University of California at Riverside,

00:03:25.839 --> 00:03:29.175
and those were the two happiest years of my life.

00:03:29.176 --> 00:03:36.649
I met people who became lifelong friends, and I took a variety of different courses...

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:38.384
I took Shakespeare and art history,

00:03:38.385 --> 00:03:41.787
as well as physical chemistry and human embryology.

00:03:41.788 --> 00:03:46.626
It was everything that Ruth Simmons, the President of Brown University,

00:03:46.627 --> 00:03:48.728
said a university should be.

00:03:48.729 --> 00:03:55.134
She said that a university should provide a profession, it should provide a philosophy of life,

00:03:55.135 --> 00:03:59.572
it should provide independence, and it should provide an awareness of the world.

00:03:59.573 --> 00:04:06.712
And the education that I got at UC Riverside did all of these things for me.

00:04:06.713 --> 00:04:11.651
After I graduated, one of my professors offered me a job in her lab,

00:04:11.652 --> 00:04:18.357
and I did research for four years.  It was there that I learned the precision of science.

00:04:18.358 --> 00:04:23.362
Afterwards, I then attended the University of California, Davis,

00:04:23.363 --> 00:04:26.999
where I pursued my doctoral degree in microbiology.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:32.205
I then did my post-doctoral research at the University of California in San Francisco

00:04:32.206 --> 00:04:35.141
and at Lawrence Berkeley Laboratory.

00:04:35.142 --> 00:04:38.377
During my professional development,

00:04:38.378 --> 00:04:42.915
I was fortunate to have met several excellent mentors.

00:04:42.916 --> 00:04:47.053
The first was Dr. Katherine Atkinson at the University of California, Riverside.

00:04:47.054 --> 00:04:52.158
The second was Dr. Merna Villarejo at the University of California, Davis.

00:04:52.159 --> 00:04:57.530
And lastly Dr. Michael Esposito at Lawrence Berkeley Laboratory.

00:04:57.531 --> 00:05:02.235
In 1993, I was hired to teach at San Francisco State University.

00:05:02.236 --> 00:05:06.672
Eventually, I received tenure and I was promoted to full professor.

00:05:06.673 --> 00:05:10.109
At San Francisco State University, I teach various

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:14.413
senior level courses in microbiology and in virology.

00:05:14.414 --> 00:05:21.521
I also do research and I use yeast to study stress responses and lipid metabolism.

00:05:21.522 --> 00:05:27.693
At San Francisco State University, I work with a wonderful, diverse set of colleagues.

00:05:27.694 --> 00:05:30.696
Of the forty professors in the biology department,

00:05:30.697 --> 00:05:36.736
8 are underrepresented minorities, and 5 are gay or lesbian.

00:05:36.737 --> 00:05:43.643
Our country and our world need an educated, diverse, and well-informed society.

00:05:43.644 --> 00:05:46.546
But we also face many challenges.

00:05:46.547 --> 00:05:51.384
Right now, we are in the midst of a severe economic crisis

00:05:51.385 --> 00:05:54.220
that makes the pursuit of an education much more difficult.

00:05:54.221 --> 00:05:59.025
But we also find ourselves in a society that is

00:05:59.026 --> 00:06:02.195
increasingly intolerant of minorities and people of color.

00:06:02.196 --> 00:06:07.867
I find this especially upsetting because I am a minority, and I'm gay.

00:06:07.868 --> 00:06:12.772
If these negative influences are allowed to escalate,

00:06:12.773 --> 00:06:16.075
we will find ourselves without people like Ruth Simmons,

00:06:16.076 --> 00:06:21.581
the first African American woman president of an Ivy League University,

00:06:21.582 --> 00:06:27.019
without Alfredo Quinones-Hinojosa, a noted neurosurgeon,

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:31.958
or without people like Luis Villarreal, an eminent virologist.

00:06:31.959 --> 00:06:34.193
We need to recognize that we need

00:06:34.194 --> 00:06:38.297
to involve all members of our society for our common good.

00:06:38.298 --> 00:06:44.103
We need to recognize the unique gift that each one of us possess.

00:06:44.104 --> 00:06:50.443
Thank you.

00:06:50.444 --> 00:06:53.179
